evaluate_daniels_catalog: predict |M|^2 on events of the arXiv:2606.23791 catalog with our
"dimensionless + coupling orders" network trained on that catalog.

Setup (once, from the repository root): the repository's CPU environment, Python 3.11, torch 2.1.2, numpy 1.26.4
    bash scripts/cpu_env.sh            # builds .venv-cpu; on a GPU machine the full requirements.txt (adds xformers)

Command:
    .venv-cpu/bin/python evaluate_daniels_catalog/evaluate.py --process "b b~ > h h" --events events.npz --out preds.npz

Inputs
  --process  the process string of the catalog's process_catalog_sm.json ("b b~ > h h") or its folder (bbx_hh).
  --events   .npz with
               momenta (N, n, 4) in GeV, (E, px, py, pz); the two incoming partons first, then the outgoing ones
                       in the catalog's order
               pdg     (n,) or (N, n) PDG ids
               alphas  (N,) each event's alpha_s (MadEvent's AQCDUP); needed unless the process has no alpha_s
               amp     (N,) optional, the true |M|^2 (then the MSE of log|M|^2 is printed)
             or one of our pools (.npy rows [momenta flat, pdg, |M|^2]) with --alphas <file.npy>.
  --n        only the first n events (default all).
  --seed     seed of the per-event Lorentz augmentation draw (default 42; the output does not depend on it, below).
Output (--out .npz, one row per input event, in the input's order)
  logM2_pred, M2_pred, sigma_logM2 (the network's sigma of log|M|^2), and logM2_true, M2_true when amp was given.

Model: model/ is runs/catalog_norm_short/DlessInOrd/sigma_stage on CC-IN2P3 (5 epochs of 1e4 events per process on
the catalog's 1352 training processes, EMA weights at the best checkpoint, then a sigma fit with the mean frozen).
diagrams.tar.gz holds the catalog's diagram files the network reads; model/lap_pe.json the diagram encoder's Laplacian
positional encodings as computed on the GPU nodes where the network reads as stored (lap_pe_table.py, FA-lxplus-86b24e3b):
every catalog diagram graph has a degenerate Laplacian eigenspace, whose eigh basis depends on the machine's
OpenBLAS kernel, and the network learned that basis. CPU and GPU both fine.

What a prediction depends on: the event alone. Each event's Lorentz augmentation is drawn from (seed, its momenta),
the frames' degeneracy noise is a hash, and the attention never crosses events (models/attention_lloca_mup.py: the
CPU path without xformers used to attend over the whole batch, which made every prediction depend on the other
events of the file and their order). Tests at the commit of this README, b b~ > h h, CPU:
  - the same 300 events in forward and reversed order, written as two files: identical predictions (max diff 0).
  - 8 augmentation seeds on 2000 events: per-event sd of log|M|^2 at most 1.7e-4 (the network is invariant).
  - against our stored zero-shot read of the same 2000 val events (catalog_short_DlessInOrd_sigstage, GPU, 2026-10-23,
    MSE of log|M|^2 0.279, mean prediction -11.265): on a GPU (CC-IN2P3, FA-ccin2p3-c3e63032) this evaluator, with the
    committed model and with the run directory, and the original scorer all give MSE 0.2791, mean -11.265: reproduced.
    On this CPU (Intel i5-10400F, .venv-cpu) with model/lap_pe.json: MSE 0.2791, per event equal to the stored read
    (rms of the difference 0.0000). Without the table, computing the encodings on this machine's OpenBLAS kernel, it
    was 0.194 lower in log|M|^2 (MSE 0.4173).
check_reproduction.py runs that comparison:
    .venv-cpu/bin/python evaluate_daniels_catalog/check_reproduction.py --pool <dc_bbx_hh val pool .npy> --stored <read.npz>
